Example 16 — `minimal` vs `standard` vs `extended` projection presets
====================================================================

This example demonstrates the three string presets accepted by
`PAOFLOW.projections(..., configuration=...)`:

  * `"minimal"`  — pseudo-atomic wavefunctions (`PP_PSWFC`) read
                   straight from each species' UPF.
  * `"standard"` — minimal valence shells from the AE/pseudo-atom
                   radial database (no polarization).
  * `"extended"` — `"standard"` augmented by a rule-based
                   polarization set (see
                   `PAOFLOW/inputs/basis_presets.py`).

The radial database is generated **on the fly from the UPF** by
`PAOFLOW.basis_gen.generate_basis_for_pseudo` (also exposed as the
`paoflow-genbasis-ps` console script).  Each subdirectory writes a
local `BASIS_PS/<Elem>/` tree; the legacy hand-curated `BASIS/`
distributed with the repo is **not** used.

Two subdirectories cover complementary regimes:

  Si_ONCV/  scalar-relativistic ONCV pseudo (insulator, fcc Si)
  Pt_REL/   fully-relativistic ONCV pseudo (metal, fcc Pt,
            noncollinear + spin-orbit; j-resolved radials)

Workflow in each subdirectory
-----------------------------
1) `pw.x < scf.in  > scf.out`
2) `pw.x < nscf.in > nscf.out`
3) `python main.py`

`main.py` is self-contained: on first run it generates `BASIS_PS/`
from the local UPF, then runs PAOFLOW three times (one per preset)
through the projections -> projectability -> pao_hamiltonian -> bands
pipeline and writes
    output_minimal/bands_0.dat
    output_standard/bands_0.dat
    output_extended/bands_0.dat
plus an overlay `bands_minimal_vs_standard_vs_extended.png`
(matplotlib optional).
